# The settings of this installation. Same rule as in the base: one line per flag, uppercased,
# hyphens to underscores, prefixed KIEBITZR_. These are merged over the base's env file, so
# only what differs belongs here.
#
# After a change, the start-up log says what actually arrived — every setting with the value in
# effect, on one line:
#   kubectl -n kiebitzr logs statefulset/kiebitzr | head -2
# A KIEBITZR_* variable that matches no flag is named in a warning beside it, which is what a
# misspelled key looks like. A malformed *value* is a start-up error and not a silently kept
# default, so a pod that stops coming up after a change here has its reason in the first log
# line.

# x86 nodes need this; the base assumes arm64. It must match the nodes, not the machine you
# run kubectl from.
KIEBITZR_PLATFORM=linux/amd64

# The vulnerability database is published once a day, so a shorter interval re-reads every
# SBOM and re-runs every match to arrive at the same findings. This repeats the default to
# make the point; drop the line if you do not want to carry it.
KIEBITZR_INTERVAL=24h

# Where the file below is mounted. The path and the ConfigMap key have to agree — that is the
# whole wiring, and getting it wrong means every image stays `unknown` rather than erroring.
KIEBITZR_BUILD_ACCESS_FILE=/etc/kiebitzr/build-access.yaml

# Check, per repository, whether an image exists that this cluster could actually deploy and
# that clears a finding. Without this the service can say "a fix is named in an advisory" and
# nothing more, and the one alert that may wake somebody cannot fire at all — it gates on this
# measurement (ADR 0011).
#
# It costs a tag listing per repository and one SBOM per candidate, so start at a small number
# and read the cost in the metrics before raising it. `-upgrade-scope` stays at `exploited`,
# the only findings that could page; `fixable` is roughly three times the work.
KIEBITZR_UPGRADE_CANDIDATES=3

# Deliberately not set here: -min-severity, -only-fixed and -ignore-file. Each makes findings
# disappear, which is a decision about what this installation wants not to see rather than a
# default worth copying — and whether such defaults are a good idea is still open in this
# project (TODO.md T6). What they drop is counted in kiebitzr_vulnerability_suppressed when
# they are set, so the number stays visible either way.
